            "text": "**SolveIt** (found at [solve.it.com](https://solve.it.com/)) is a dual-purpose initiative consisting of a **software platform** and a **problem-solving methodology** designed to help people use Artificial Intelligence to become better coders, writers, and thinkers.\n\nIt was created by **Jeremy Howard** (founder of fast.ai) and **Eric Ries** (author of *The Lean Startup*).\n\n### 1. The \"SolveIt Method\"\nThe core philosophy is based on George Pólya’s famous 1945 book, *How to Solve It*. Rather than \"outsourcing\" your thinking to an AI (e.g., asking ChatGPT to write an entire app at once), the SolveIt method encourages:\n*   **Small Steps:** Breaking problems into tiny, manageable increments.\n*   **Immediate Feedback:** Writing 1–2 lines of code and seeing the results instantly.\n*   **Human Agency:** Using AI as a collaborator to explore ideas and fix bugs while the human maintains \"craftsmanship\" and total control over the logic.\n\n### 2. The SolveIt Platform\nThe platform is a private, cloud-based Linux development and writing environment. It is described as a hybrid tool that combines the best features of:\n*   **Jupyter Notebooks** (for interactive coding)\n*   **ChatGPT/Claude** (for AI assistance)\n*   **Cursor/Claude Code** (for AI-integrated editing)\n*   **Full Linux Terminal** (for system administration and devops)\n\nIt is designed to \"maximize learning\" by showing the user exactly how a solution is found, rather than just providing a finished product.\n\n### 3. The Course\nThe initiative is currently centered around a **5-week (10-lesson) course** taught by Howard and Ries. Key details include:\n*   **Topics:** Coding puzzles, data science (NumPy/PyTorch), web programming (FastHTML), long-form writing, and building startups.\n*   **Cost:** The course is typically priced at **$500**, which includes 30 days of platform access. Afterward, users can subscribe to the platform for **$10/month**.\n*   **Community:** Access includes a dedicated Discord community for support.\n\n### 4. Who is it for?\nWhile popular with experienced software engineers and AI researchers, it is marketed toward anyone who wants to build things with AI—including students, writers, and entrepreneurs—provided they have a basic comfort level with Python.\n\n**Current Status:** Signups for the course often run in \"cohorts.\" As of the latest update on their site, signups may be periodically closed between course cycles, but users can join a mailing list for future openings.",
