            "text": "Otters are semi-aquatic carnivorous mammals belonging to the weasel family (**mustelids**). There are 13 different species found all over the world, ranging from small river otters to the large sea otter.\n\n### Key Facts:\n*   **Physical Features:** They have long, slim bodies, webbed feet for swimming, and powerful tails. Sea otters have the **thickest fur** of any animal—up to one million hairs per square inch—which keeps them warm since they lack blubber.\n*   **Diet:** They are skilled hunters that primarily eat fish, crabs, mussels, and frogs. Some species use rocks as \"tools\" to crack open shellfish.\n*   **Habitat:** They live in both freshwater (rivers and lakes) and marine environments (coastal oceans).\n*   **Behavior:** Known for being playful, they often slide down muddy banks or play with stones. Sea otters are famous for **holding hands** while sleeping to keep from drifting apart in the current.\n*   **Conservation:** Most otter species are currently considered threatened or endangered due to habitat loss, pollution, and historical hunting for their fur.",
